    <thead><tr><th>Vehicle</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {%- for v in peer_benchmarks.vehicles %}
      <tr><td>{{ v.label }}</td><td>{{ v.count }}</td></tr>
      {%- endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>On blended-finance structure: <strong>{{ peer_benchmarks.bfCapitalStack }} of {{ peer_benchmarks.total }}</strong> funds have a publicly-disclosed capital stack with named tranches, and <strong>{{ peer_benchmarks.bfParallelTA }} of {{ peer_benchmarks.total }}</strong> publicly disclose a parallel technical-assistance facility. For funds where the structure is public, common patterns: junior / mezzanine / senior debt stacks anchored by a DFI or development-bank first-loss layer; standalone TA facilities of $1M–$10M sitting alongside the main vehicle, often donor-funded.</p>

  <p><em>Impact-specific implications of the vehicle choice — drafted via the <code>market-researcher:sector-overview</code> skill against the INGO impact-fund cohort:</em></p>

  <p>Closed-end ({{ peer_benchmarks.vehicles[0].count }} of the cohort) dominates, but the choice has impact-specific implications that aren't usually surfaced. Closed-end vehicles force exit decisions on a fund timeline that may not align with impact horizons — especially for smallholder, last-mile, or infrastructure portfolios where the impact thesis depends on a 10-15 year holding period that LP commitments don't naturally support. Evergreen vehicles ({{ peer_benchmarks.vehicles[1].count }} in this cohort) avoid that misalignment by recycling capital into mission-aligned deals without GP-LP renegotiation, and have grown in share as impact GPs have realized closed-end is the wrong default for portfolios with long impact-realization periods. Blended-finance vehicles ({{ peer_benchmarks.vehicles[3].count }} explicit, plus {{ peer_benchmarks.bfCapitalStack }} with disclosed multi-tranche capital stacks) are an architectural commitment, not a tactical choice — they require a willing first-loss provider and an LP base that understands the seniority logic. DIB structures (2 in the cohort) remain rare because outcomes-based payment requires both rigorous outcomes measurement and an outcomes-payer with budget — operationally heavy and not the right starting point for a first-time team.</p>

