  <h2>Fund economics: the decisions you have to make</h2>
  <p><em>Drafted via the <code>market-researcher:sector-overview</code> skill against the INGO impact-fund cohort. Frames the management-fee, carry, hurdle, and GP-commit decisions as positioning logic for first-time INGO impact GPs.</em></p>

  <p>The fee, carry, hurdle, and GP-commit decisions a first-time impact GP has to make are less about benchmarks and more about positioning. The first decision is impact-first vs finance-first: an impact-first fund explicitly accepts below-market financial returns as the compact with the LP and prices accordingly — fees can be defended at parity with commercial impact funds because the LP isn't buying market returns, but carry and hurdles often look closer to a credit fund or evergreen vehicle than to a buyout fund. A finance-first impact fund prices like its commercial peers and competes on impact additionality, not on concessional terms. This positioning is upstream of every subsequent fund-economics decision and an INGO board often hasn't decided it explicitly — the GP team frequently has to force the question.</p>

  <p>The second decision is downstream of the anchor LP. DFI anchors typically push toward concessional fee terms and lower hurdles in exchange for the credibility and follow-on capital they bring; large foundation anchors more often expect parity with commercial impact funds and treat below-market pricing as a separate (and harder-to-justify) negotiation. Family office anchors vary widely. The implication: if the anchor LP isn't yet identified, fee/carry decisions are premature. The third reality is GP commit — the standard "1-2% of fund size from the GP team's personal capital" is rare for INGO-transitioning teams. The parent INGO sometimes contributes as proxy GP-commit (with the governance complexity that creates); other teams negotiate fee-deferral as commit substitute; some LPs accept carry-only structures with no cash GP-commit at all if the team's track record and parental backing are strong enough.</p>

